    description: "FOUND 2026-10-21 while sizing WI-nilir, on dev e926fc1045.\n\nA nested function that reads a variable assigned AFTER the nested function is defined gets no DDG edge from that assignment, because reaching definitions follow CFG order and the nested body is a may-run region hung off its definition (WI-faful). The closure reads the variable's value at CALL time. So the walk sees the secret's only use as the terminating console.log, exhausts, and REMOVES the flow.\n\nRepro (verify-claims, claim host_secret -> network):\n    function main() {\n      let u = \"http://x/\";\n      function setup() { u = u + k; }\n      const k = process.env.K;\n      console.log(k.length);\n      setup();\n      fetch(u);\n    }\ndev: SECRET-NET verdict=confirmed, no evidence (walk main [4] -> [7] = False). The value reaches fetch at run time.\nSame body with setup defined after k: ddg_mixed/escaped (k(4) -> uses 6,7 inside the closure). Python twin (box[\"u\"] += k in a nested def defined before k): ddg_mixed/escaped on dev, for an unrelated reason (the subscript), so not a measured false clean in Python.\n\nInvariant: every definition of a variable that a nested function reads reaches that read in the DDG, or the variable is unaccounted (the walk may not refute a walk carrying it).\n\nFix shape: after the solve, for each nested-function region in the body, each variable read in it and each definition of that variable OUTSIDE the region: no DDG edge from that definition into the region means the read is one the DDG cannot place -> add the variable to unaccounted_names (INV-lupav L4, variable granularity). Bears on WI-nilir: at module scope top-level defs usually precede the module code they read (py: def setup(): global URL; URL += K ... K = getenv(); setup(); get(URL)), so module-scope refutation is unsound without this."  # 0b9c
